course: books
lesson: 67
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XI, part 3: «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears» (loyal friends, cold pie and «Be quiet at once!»)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: «What's become of them, the dear fellows? I had forgotten all about them,» says Toad lightly about Mole and Badger. The Rat tells him off: while Toad rode about in cars and on horses, those two loyal friends have been camping out in all weathers, watching his house and planning to get it back. Toad sobs that he will go out into the dark night to share their hardships — then hears the chink of dishes and cries «Supper's here at last, hooray!»
explain: After supper there is a heavy knock: Badger, muddy and rough from nights outdoors. He shakes Toad's paw — «Welcome home, Toad! Alas! what am I saying? Home, indeed!» — then turns his back and eats a large slice of cold pie. The Rat whispers that Badger is always gloomy when he is hungry.
explain: Then Mole arrives, shabby, with hay and straw in his fur, and dances round Toad calling him clever and ingenious. That is all Toad needs: he puffs up and boasts about the prison, the train, the disguises, and shows a handful of silver from «horse-dealing». Soon Rat, Mole and Toad are all shouting at once about what Toad ought to do.
explain: A thin, dry voice says «Be quiet at once, all of you!» — and there is silence. Badger finishes his cheese, stands by the fire and asks Toad what his father, Badger's old friend, would have said. Toad rolls over sobbing. Badger is kinder then: let bygones be bygones. The stoats are the best sentries in the world and the Hall can't be taken by force — but «there are more ways of getting back a place than taking it by storm». And now he will tell them a great secret.
fact: Toad on Mole and Badger: «What's become of them, the dear fellows? I had forgotten all about them.»
fact: The Rat: they have been «camping out in the open, in every sort of weather, living very rough by day and lying very hard by night; watching over your house, patrolling your boundaries».
fact: «You don't deserve to have such true and loyal friends, Toad, you don't, really.» (loyal = faithful, sticking by you)
fact: Toad sobs «Let me go out and find them, out into the cold, dark night... Hold on a bit! Surely I heard the chink of dishes on a tray!» — his tears stop the moment supper comes.
fact: The Rat makes «large allowances» because Toad had been «on prison fare» (prison food) for a long time (to make allowances = to forgive someone because of their situation).
fact: Badger arrives muddy and «rough and touzled» (untidy, tousled); «Welcome home, Toad! Alas! what am I saying? Home, indeed! This is a poor home-coming.»
fact: The Rat: «He's always rather low and despondent when he's wanting his victuals. In half an hour's time he'll be quite a different animal.» (despondent = gloomy; victuals = food, said «vittles»)
fact: Mole comes in «very shabby and unwashed, with bits of hay and straw sticking in his fur», and praises Toad: «you clever, ingenious, intelligent Toad!» — while the Rat pulls him by the elbow to stop him, too late.
fact: Toad puffs up: «I'm not really clever, according to my friends. I've only broken out of the strongest prison in England, that's all!»
fact: He shows a handful of silver: «And how do you think I done it, Mole? Horse-dealing! That's how I done it!» («I done it» is wrong grammar; it should be «I did it».)
fact: Mole reports: «Sentries posted everywhere, guns poked out at us, stones thrown at us... and when they see us, my! how they do laugh! That's what annoys me most!»
fact: When all three shout at once, Badger says «Be quiet at once, all of you!» and «instantly every one was silent»; they wait until he has finished his cheese.
fact: Badger: «What do you think your father, my old friend, would have said if he had been here to-night, and had known of all your goings on?»
fact: Badger: «We're going to let bygones be bygones, and try and turn over a new leaf.» (let bygones be bygones = forget past quarrels; turn over a new leaf = start behaving better)
fact: Toad: «I shall go and enlist for a soldier» (enlist = join up). Badger: «There are more ways of getting back a place than taking it by storm.» (taking by storm = attacking with force)
fact: Badger: «I haven't said my last word yet. Now I'm going to tell you a great secret» — one that Toad's father told him and «wouldn't have dreamt of telling» his own son, because Toad «simply cannot hold his tongue» (cannot keep a secret).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «hangry» = cross because hungry; «lowkey» = a bit, quietly; «bostin'» (Black Country) = great; «chur» (New Zealand) = thanks; «hun» (UK) = dear, love.
exercise: What did Toad say he had done about Mole and Badger? | Forgotten all about them | Written to them from prison every single week | Sent them money | Worried about them every day
exercise: Where had Mole and Badger been living lately? | Camping out in the open in all weathers | At the Rat's house, warm and dry | In Badger's house in the Wild Wood, with the hedgehogs | At an inn
exercise: What were Mole and Badger doing out there? | Watching Toad's house and planning to get it back | Looking for Toad in every prison in England | Fishing | Hiding from the police, who were still looking for anyone who knew Toad
exercise: «You don't deserve to have such true and loyal friends.» What does «loyal» mean? | Faithful, sticking by you | Royal, like a king or queen | Rich, with plenty of money to spend | Clever at making plans to fight the weasels
exercise: What stopped Toad's tears about his friends' hardships? | The chink of dishes — supper had come | Badger knocked | Rat hit him | He fell asleep in his chair by the fire
exercise: Why did the Rat make «large allowances» for Toad's greed? | He had been on prison food for a long time | He was always greedy | He was ill with a cold from the river | It was his birthday that day, and birthdays mean extra helpings
exercise: Who knocked first, heavily? | Badger | The Mole | Otter, with a fish | The ferret from the gate
exercise: «Welcome home, Toad! Alas! what am I saying? Home, indeed!» Why does Badger correct himself? | Toad's real home has been taken, so it is a poor home-coming | Badger forgot whose house it was | Toad has no home at all, because he sold Toad Hall to buy cars | It is Badger's own house
exercise: What did Badger eat first? | A large slice of cold pie | A big lump of yellow cheese | Bread and butter with a cup of tea | Fried bacon and eggs from the pan
exercise: Why, says the Rat, is Badger so gloomy? | He is always low when he is wanting his victuals | He is angry with Toad | He is ill | He has lost his house in the Wild Wood to the stoats as well
exercise: «Wanting his victuals.» What are «victuals»? | Food | Clothes | Friends | Sleep
exercise: How did Mole look when he came in? | Shabby and unwashed, with hay and straw in his fur | Smart in a new coat and a hat with a feather | Wet through from swimming | Hurt and bandaged all over after another fight
exercise: «You clever, ingenious, intelligent Toad!» What did the Rat try to do when Mole said this? | Pull him by the elbow to stop him | Clap | Agree | Laugh loudly and say Toad was the cleverest
exercise: «I've only broken out of the strongest prison in England, that's all!» Why does Toad say «that's all»? | He is pretending to be modest while really boasting | He means it was easy and anybody could have done it | He is sad | He is reading from a newspaper report
exercise: What did Toad pull out of his pocket? | A handful of silver | A gold watch on a chain | The barge-woman's soap | The key of Toad Hall, which he had kept safe in prison
exercise: «Horse-dealing! That's how I done it!» What is the correct grammar? | «That's how I did it» | «That's how I doned it» | «That's how I done it» | «That's how I have did it»
exercise: What annoyed Mole most about the sentries? | They laughed at him and Badger | Their guns | Their stones | They never slept, day or night, so there was no chance at all
exercise: What happened when Rat, Mole and Toad all talked at once? | A thin, dry voice said «Be quiet at once, all of you!» | Badger fell asleep | The stoats heard them all the way from Toad Hall and came running | Toad ran out of the room again to fight
exercise: What did Badger reach for after silencing everyone? | The cheese | His heavy stick | His pistols, to check they were loaded | A map of the Hall
exercise: Whom did Badger mention to make Toad ashamed? | Toad's father, Badger's old friend | The magistrates | The barge-woman | The gaoler's daughter, who had helped him so kindly
exercise: «Let bygones be bygones.» What does this mean? | Forget past quarrels | Say goodbye to each other | Go away and start again | Remember every bad thing anybody has ever done
exercise: What did Badger say about the stoats? | They make the best sentries in the world | They are lazy | They sleep at night | They are afraid of badgers and will run at once
exercise: What did Toad say he would do if the Hall was lost? | Enlist as a soldier | Go back to prison | Live with the Rat | Buy a new house far away in Town
exercise: «There are more ways of getting back a place than taking it by ...» what? | Storm | Night | Force of numbers and guns | Boat
test: Lily, lowkey (quietly) shocked: «Toad forgot his mates who were sleeping rough for him?!» Is she right? | Yes: he said «I had forgotten all about them» | No: he asked about them the very first thing when he arrived, and cried | No: he had sent them a letter | Yes, but they forgot him too
test: Why is it funny when Toad cries «Supper's here at last, hooray!»? | A second before, he was sobbing that he'd go out into the cold night for his friends | He hates supper and always says so | Supper was very late that night because the Rat was busy | He was not hungry
test: Noah, hangry (cross because hungry) himself: «Badger was rude to Toad, he just turned his back.» What did the Rat explain? | Badger is always gloomy when hungry and will be a different animal after eating | Badger hates Toad now and will never speak to him again | Badger was deaf | Badger was sleepy
test: Why does the Rat pull Mole's elbow when Mole calls Toad clever? | Praise makes Toad puff up and boast | Mole was being rude to Badger | Mole was about to fall into the fire from dancing so wildly | Rat wanted the pie
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Badger shouts very loudly to stop the argument.» | False: it is a thin, dry voice — and everyone goes silent | True: he roars like a lion | True: he bangs the table with his great fist until the plates jump | False: he says nothing
test: What would have happened if Toad had tried to take the Hall by force? | The stoat sentries, the best in the world, would have beaten him again | He would have won easily | The weasels would have run away at once and given the Hall back without a fight | Badger would have paid
test: Why does Badger mention Toad's father? | His father was Badger's old friend, and Toad has let the family name down | His father is coming to visit tonight from the other side of the river | His father owned the Wild Wood | His father was a policeman
test: Ellie, «bostin'» (great) news!: «Badger's gonna help after all, innit?» What shows he has a plan? | «I haven't said my last word yet. Now I'm going to tell you a great secret.» | «Be quiet at once, all of you!» — he just wants peace and quiet to eat his supper | «Unhappy Toad!» | «Alas! what am I saying?»
test: Mason, «chur» (thanks): «so Badger stayed mad at Toad all night?» Put it right. | After the scolding he spoke more kindly: let bygones be bygones | Yes, he never forgave him | He laughed at him | He left in a temper and went back to sleep in the cold field
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, how did Badger welcome Rat and Mole lost in the snow? | Gruff at first, then kind: fire, supper, dressing-gowns, beds | He sent them away into the snow and told them never to come back | He laughed at them | He gave them a map
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, what did Badger say he would do about Toad «when once the year has really turned»? | Take Toad seriously in hand, with Rat and Mole | Buy Toad's car | Leave Toad alone to do whatever he liked, as a free animal should | Report Toad to the police
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Badger talked to Toad alone in the smoking-room for three-quarters of an hour. What happened after? | Toad said he'd promised «in there», but outside he wasn't sorry at all | Toad was cured for ever and never touched a car again | Toad ran away at once | Toad punched Badger
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, Toad told the gaoler's daughter about Toad Hall. What did he say first? | That it was «an eligible self-contained gentleman's residence» — like an estate agent's advert | That it was small and cold, with a leaking roof and no dinners | That it was haunted | That it belonged to Badger
test: Looking back: in chapter V, Mole cried by the roadside. What did the Rat do then? | He turned back in the dark and helped Mole find his old home | He told him to stop crying and walk faster because snow was coming | He went home alone | He fetched Badger
test: How has Mole changed since chapter I, when he was new and shy? | He now camps out, guards the Hall and stands up to sentries | He is shyer than ever and hides whenever anyone knocks | He has left the river | He hates Toad
test: ★★★ Badger says Toad's father told him a secret «he wouldn't have dreamt of telling you». Why might a father NOT tell his own son? | Because the son «simply cannot hold his tongue» | Because the son was still a baby, far too young to understand | Because the father forgot | Because it was a lie
test: ★★★ Count the animals in the Rat's room at the end of this part. | 4: Rat, Toad, Badger and Mole | 3: Rat, Toad and Badger | 5, with Otter, who came in from the river | 2: Rat and Toad
test: ★★★ «In half an hour's time he'll be quite a different animal.» What kind of «different» does the Rat mean? | In a better mood, not a different kind of creature | Turned into a bird, like Toad nearly was | Asleep | Gone home
exam: Identify the remark by which Toad disclosed his neglect of his two friends. | «I had forgotten all about them» | «Let me go out and find them, out into the cold, dark night» | «I am an ungrateful beast» | «Supper's here at last»
exam: Ascertain the explanation proffered by the Rat for the Badger's despondent demeanour upon arrival. | That the Badger was invariably low-spirited when in want of victuals | That he disapproved of Toad's return and wished him back in gaol | That he was indisposed | That he had been defeated by the stoats that evening
exam: It is given that three animals were speaking simultaneously. State the means by which order was restored. | The Badger commanded silence in a thin, dry voice | The Rat rang a bell and demanded order in the manner of a chairman | The Toad fainted | The Mole departed
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Badger's assessment of an assault upon Toad Hall. | That it was useless, the stoats being too strong and the best sentinels in the world | That it should be undertaken at once, before the sentinels were reinforced | That the police should conduct it | That the weasels would surrender without resistance
